<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John J. Pershing</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1860">1860</a>–<a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as John Joseph Pershing, Black Jack Pershing</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John J. Pershing was an American army officer who served as military governor of the Moro Province in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> from 1909 to 1913, where he disarmed the population and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bud-bagsak">Battle of Bud Bagsak</a>. He later led the Punitive Expedition into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1916 and commanded the American Expeditionary Forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, becoming General of the Armies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-j-pershing.jpg" alt="John J. Pershing" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bain News Service, publisher · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_John_Joseph_Pershing_head_on_shoulders.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John Joseph Pershing was born on a farm near Laclede, Missouri, on 13 September 1860, the son of the farmer and store owner John Fletcher Pershing and Ann Elizabeth Thompson. He became a West Point cadet in July 1882 and rose to First Captain, the highest cadet rank. He served with the 6th Cavalry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a> and South Dakota and was cited for bravery against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a>. As a West Point instructor from 1897 he was unpopular for his strictness, and the nickname Black Jack that cadets gave him stayed with him for life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the Spanish–American War Pershing fought on Kettle and San Juan Hills in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and was cited for gallantry; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a> called him the coolest man under fire he had ever seen. In 1940 he was awarded the Distinguished Service Cross for leading an assault against Moro defenders at Mount Bagsak, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolo">Jolo</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, on 15 June 1913. On 27 August 1915 a fire at the Presidio in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> killed his wife, Frances Warren Pershing, and three of their daughters; only his son Warren survived. On 15 March 1916 he led an expedition of 10,000 men some 350 miles (560 km) into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pancho-villa">Pancho Villa</a>, which routed Villa's followers but failed to capture him.</p><p>President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a> chose Pershing to command the American Expeditionary Forces, and he was installed on 10 May 1917, with headquarters at Chaumont. He resisted British and French demands that American troops serve as replacements in their armies, although in March 1918, when General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-petain">Philippe Pétain</a> said he had few reserves left, he put all available American divisions at Pétain's disposal. American soldiers fought at Cantigny, Château-Thierry and Belleau Wood in June 1918, and at St. Mihiel in September the First Army, under his direct command, overwhelmed a German salient. In the Meuse-Argonne Offensive he kept about 600,000 soldiers in hard fighting for 47 days.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In September 1919 Congress authorised the promotion of Pershing to General of the Armies, a rank created for him. His memoirs, My Experiences in the World War, won the 1932 Pulitzer Prize for history. Historians have criticised his reliance on costly frontal assaults, and his failure to halt attacks in the last hours before the armistice of 11 November 1918; he said he followed the orders of Ferdinand Foch, and Congress found that no one was culpable. He mentored many later generals, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-c-marshall">George C. Marshall</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Dwight D. Eisenhower</a>. Pershing died on 15 July 1948 and was buried in Arlington National Cemetery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John J. Pershing” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Governors Leonard Wood, Tasker Bliss and <b>John J. Pershing</b> ruled with a legislative council, abolishing slavery, imposing a head tax and courts, and building schools and roads.</blockquote><a href="/state/moro-province">Moro Province</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Laclede, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.79,-93.17&amp;year=1917" aria-label="Laclede on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John J. Pershing"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4792 4164h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4792 4164h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.79,-93.17&amp;year=1917">What was here in 1917? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
